{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"agent_message","text":"DO NOT LAND\n\n- **P1-a — not closed.** The revised `origin-of` preserves an incoming origin when an output address is found in the input packet, but it still classifies provenance by content address rather than occurrence. If a macro generates an application identical to an input application, the two share an address and the generated occurrence is assigned the input’s origin. The same limitation prevents distinguishing retained occurrences with different origins that share an address. See [macro.cljc:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1087), [macro.cljc:1140](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1140), and [macro.cljc:1200](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1200).\n\n- **P1-b — not closed.** The namespace-global counter is removed, and an explicitly scoped allocator is available. But the default token is derived from module name and tree root, so independent admissions of the same specification receive the same origin, including across processes and restarts. The code treats those calls as a replay without a composition-supplied admission identity establishing that they are one recorded event. The DHT publication entry point uses this default. See [publish.cljc:147](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:147), [publish.cljc:277](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:277), and [dht.cljc:45](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc:45).\n\n- **P2 — partially closed.** Declared tables and per-occurrence-origin validation now reject multiple origins with a diagnostic containing the occurrence and both origins. The default validator mode skips that check; it rejects the foreign-origin row as a nonexistent path instead, without naming both claims. See [clojure.cljc:204](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:204) and [clojure.cljc:221](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:221).\n\n- **Oracle and closure checks.** The diff does not change the oracle consumer; it still reads the site table and stamps compiled sites at [latte_oracle_test.clj:2394](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2394) and [latte_oracle_test.clj:2464](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2464). Closure walking still reads both manifest references, and the stale comment has been corrected: [closure.cljc:195](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc:195).\n\n- **New finding — scope ID collisions.** The scoped allocator discards keyword namespaces, so distinct caller-chosen IDs such as `:a/publisher` and `:b/publisher` produce the same token at the same ordinal; a string with the same name also collides. See [publish.cljc:140](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:140).\n\nNo tests were run, as requested."}}
